# tools/dpu_sigma_patch_test.py, Desktop 2 (DESKTOP-CBL5NUA), runs 3 and 4, 2026-10-12.
# Run 3 started 20261012T191325Z UTC, run 4 started 20261012T192406Z UTC.
# Same method as runs 1-2 (results/aie/dpu_sigma_patch_desktop2_20261012T182840Z.log): Ryzen AI 1.7.1 VitisAI EP,
# resnet_env17, phoenix 4x4.xclbin; the 32-channel single-Conv fixture of tools/xint8_arithmetic_probe.py compiled
# --fresh at shift_cut 16, shift_bias 0; the CACHED xmodel edited and reloaded without --fresh. Every arm: idle-device
# check first, the EP must leave the edited xmodel byte-identical (it did, every arm, both runs), the Conv must be
# on the NPU (it was, every arm), and NPU output codes are scored exactly against named integer models.
# New in these runs: byte 0x7B (shift_bias << 2 | 1) is edited too, and negative shift_cut is written as its byte.
# Arm notation sc:sb = shift_cut byte value : shift_bias. sigma printed as shift_cut + 14 (Theorem 1 bookkeeping).
# Run 3 was produced by the tool BEFORE models code6_sb4 / code6_sb4_sat / sigma6_sb4 were added (they were fitted
# on run 3's outputs); its arm lines below list only the models that existed then. The rescore section re-scores
# the same saved outputs with the final tool. Run 4 was produced by the final tool.
# Compiled xmodels and output arrays are AMD-compiled artefacts and stay in git-ignored scratch/.
#
# ===== RUN 3 =====
# Pre-registration (sha256 ca48cd0d8dd3b1f489f59b43274a81bfe7d3155e090bae027420a864e3785fb7):
#   written_utc 2026-10-12T19:13:13Z, before any 0x7B-patched or negative-shift xmodel has been loaded
#   context: run 2 (prereg 9c119781...) patched REG_0 byte 0x7C alone at shift_bias 0. sigma 31..33 EXACT; 46,47,49 predicted vsrs left shifts, read all zero point (miss).
#            Its fixture's accumulator caps near 2^19, so every shift >= 20 was degenerate there.
#   test: tools/dpu_sigma_patch_test.py --site reg0 --base 16 --base-sb 0
#     --controls 15,1,16:8,16:15,10:12
#     --patches 16:17,16:20,17:17,18:19,19:19,20:20,22:22,24:23,26:23,28:23,30:24,31:24,32:24,40:24,46:24,47:24,49:24,50:24,63:24,64:24,128:24,255:24,-1,-2,-3,-8,-14,31:26,31:28,40:38
#   arm = shift_cut:shift_bias. 0x7C = shift_cut & 0xff, 0x7B = shift_bias<<2|1. A large shift_bias puts 127<<sb into the accumulator, which is what makes large shifts observable.
#   separation table (elements, of 57344, by which each alternative model differs from exact; from --dry-run, same tool): prereg3_dry.txt, sha256 4babb76fd23847799421d76a6d312a9c52354e8659b3d59825f61ebf2a440ac3
#   
#   decision rules:
#   - An arm is EXACT if H_exact has 0 mismatches. Every arm also lists which named models match.
#   - VOID if any control (15:0, 1:0, 16:8, 16:15, 10:12) is not EXACT. 16:8, 16:15 and 10:12 are the first test of the 0x7B site;
#     they differ from base by 2715 / 28672 / 47848 elements.
#   - REJECTED if the EP rewrites the xmodel. A hang or device error is recorded, not retried.
#   
#   predictions:
#   P1 out-of-contract shift_bias, in-contract shift_cut: 16:17, 16:20 -> EXACT (sb_clamp15 and base differ by 43008 each).
#   P2 joint grid, sb <= 23 so a 32-bit accumulator holds it: 17:17, 18:19, 19:19, 20:20, 22:22, 24:23, 26:23, 28:23, 30:24 -> EXACT.
#      Separates sc_clamp16/17 (>= 29696), sigma_mask5 (>= 39895 from 18:19 on), sb_clamp15 (>= 14336).
#      Does NOT separate sc_clamp31 or vsrs6 (identical below shift_cut 32).
#   P3 large shift_cut at sb 24 (bias up to 2^31): 31:24 -> EXACT. 32:24, 40:24, 46:24, 47:24, 49:24, 50:24, 63:24, 64:24 -> EXACT
#      (all zero point), meaning an effective shift of at least 32. Separates sc_clamp31 (14336), vsrs6 at 46..50 (54231),
#      sigma_mask5, sc_clamp16/17. LIMIT: a clamp anywhere in 32..45, or any shift >= 32, cannot be told from exact on a
#      32-bit accumulator. This narrows run 2's miss; it cannot explain it.
#   P4 byte semantics: 128:24, 255:24 -> EXACT (byte read unsigned; signed differs by 54231).
#      -1, -2, -3, -8, -14 (bytes ff fe fd f8 f2, sb 0) -> NOT EXACT, matching 'unsigned' (unsigned differs from exact by 54184;
#      sc_floor0 differs by 25958). This follows from P4's first half: a byte cannot be both. If 255:24 reads unsigned, -1 must too.
#      Pre-registered as a consistency check, not an independent finding.
#   P5 accumulator width probes (bias past 2^31; no "exact" claim): 31:26, 31:28, 40:38. Each reports which of exact (wide) /
#      sat32 / wrap32 matches; each differs from exact by 14336. Prediction: sat32 or wrap32 (a 32-bit accumulator, as the
#      DPU's int8 path is believed to be), not wide. A match to none is reported as such.
#   
#   Note: the sigma-0 row in BENCHMARKS (2026-09-09) ran on CPU fallback; any negative-shift arm here is the first DPU data at the lower edge.
